## Tutorials

You can also pass a structure to a function.

This is useful when you want to 
work with grouped data inside a function:

Note:Since the structure is passed by value, the function 
gets a copy of the structure.

This means that the original data is not changed.

You can also pass a structure by reference, using&.

This allows the function to modify the original data:

Tip:Use reference if you want the function to change the structure's data, or to avoid copying large structures.

### Code Examples

```
struct Car {
string brand;
int year;
};
void myFunction(Car 
    c) {
cout << "Brand: " << c.brand << ", Year: " << c.year << "\n";
}
int main() {
Car myCar = {"Toyota", 2020};
myFunction(myCar);
return 0;
}

---

struct Car {
string brand;
int year;
};
void updateYear(Car &c) {
c.year++;
}
int main() {
Car myCar = {"Toyota", 2020};
updateYear(myCar);
cout << "The " << myCar.brand << " is now from 
    year " << myCar.year << ".\n";
return 0;
}
```

## Tutorials

To demonstrate a practical example of using functions, let's create a program that converts a value from fahrenheit to celsius:

### Code Examples

```
// Function to convert 
  Fahrenheit to Celsius
float toCelsius(float fahrenheit) {
return 
  (5.0 / 9.0) * (fahrenheit - 32.0);
}
int main() {
// Set a 
  fahrenheit value
float f_value = 98.8;
// Call the 
  function with the fahrenheit value
float result = 
  toCelsius(f_value);
// Print the fahrenheit value
cout 
  << "Fahrenheit: " << f_value << "\n";
// Print the result
cout << "Convert Fahrenheit to Celsius: " << result << "\n";
return 0;
}
```

